  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA warm front pushing through the area has brought mainly showers but \nalso a few embedded thunderstorm this morning. Trailing showers, \nstratiform rain driven by PVA aloft continue this afternoon and \nearly evening when activity should overall be on the wane across the \nCWA. Potential fog into the overnight hours developing late where it \nrained today but could be inhibited by lingering clouds in the \neastern CWA, so this could end up being more of western CWA issue. \nBrief warm sector residence time into Sunday when a cold front \nenters from the northwest. Low/mid level frontogenesis should be on \nthe increase as the front is tracking through, and see these \nincreases in the southeastern zones of the CWA. That is when a line \nof convection with the front is expected to form, likely just south \nand east of the lakeshore areas east of Cleveland associated with a \nsmall pocket of a low level jet maximum and increasing instability \nfrom dewpoints near 70F at the surface and a 3-5C surge at 850mb \ncoinciding with the aforementioned low level jet. Sufficient 0-6km \nshear should organize storms into a line ahead of the cold front. \nThere is a potential for severe as a result, but ultimately, these \nshould exit the CWA fairly quickly with forward motion to the \nsoutheast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nVery cool day Monday in a much cooler airmass behind the late Sunday \ncold front, with 70F tough to come by for the eastern half of the \nCWA. This will be short-lived, however, with Canadian high pressure \nthat was over the area Monday shifting quickly eastward and back \ninto return flow and quickly warming temperatures again through \nmidweek. Warm front likely to set up along the fetch of Lake Erie or \nnear it into the same time frame, bringing unsettled weather back \nwith dewpoints up from the lower 50s to possibly the lower 70s and \nair temperatures into the mid 80s. Expecting unsettled weather as a \nresult with off and on convection from Tuesday night through at \nleast Thursday for the area.",
